    {"claim": "Frontier systems with the greatest catastrophic potential are deployed by firms with market capitalizations above $100 billion, not by garage startups.", "targets": []},
    {"claim": "Congress should restrict the state-of-the-art defense for frontier developers, because the entities best positioned to assess novel risk are precisely the ones currently insulated from its costs.", "targets": []},
    {"claim": "The policy question is not whether to impose liability, but how to calibrate it so that Anthropic and a three-person startup face proportionate obligations tied to what they actually deploy.", "targets": []}
  ],
  "key_assumptions": [
    {"assumption": "Competitive pressure systematically erodes safety investment absent binding external constraint, as demonstrated across aviation, pharmaceuticals, and financial derivatives.", "if_wrong": "If AI labs sustained meaningful safety investment under pure market discipline, the case for strict liability would weaken toward a disclosure-and-audit regime rather than a liability spine."},
    {"assumption": "A meaningful subset of AI harms — bioweapon uplift, critical infrastructure compromise, large-scale manipulation — are categorically irreversible and cannot be remedied through ex post damages.", "if_wrong": "If all foreseeable AI harms are remediable through compensation, pre-deployment gates become disproportionate and liability alone would suffice as the governance instrument."}
