arket capitalizations above $100 billion, not by garage startups. Liability should scale with capability and deployment criticality — a model fine-tuned for hospital triage faces different obligations than a chatbot generating recipes. Crucially, Congress should restrict the 'state-of-the-art' defense for frontier developers, because the entities best positioned to assess novel risk are precisely the ones currently insulated from its costs. This is the same logic the Supreme Court applied in Beshada v. Johns-Manville: superior knowledge cannot become a liability shield.\n\nDiffuse causation across AI supply chains is the accountability vacuum that only strict liability can close. When a foundation model from one developer is fine-tuned by a second firm, deployed by a third, and interacts with another AI agent to produce harm, traditional negligence regimes collapse into finger-pointing. The SolarWinds compromise demonstrated how software supply chains diffuse responsibility until no one is accountable; AI systems multiply this problem because they compose and interact in ways their developers did not specify. Strict liability with joint-and-several allocation across the supply chain forces participants to contract for safety internally, the same mechanism that made the Comprehensive Environmental Response, Compensation, and Liability Act (CERCLA) effective for toxic waste.\n\nThe strongest opposing position deserves a fair hearing: accelerationists argue that liability uncertainty itself chills beneficial deployment, that medical AI which could save lives sits unused because developers fear unbounded exposure, and that Europe's AI Act already demonstrates how compliance costs entrench incumbents. That argument has real force for moderate-risk applications, which is why graduated liability tied to capability thresholds — not uniform strict liability — is the correct design. But the argument fails for frontier systems, where the developer's information advantage is la