  "statement": "Strict liability is necessary but structurally incomplete, and the binding constraint on accountability is not liability's chill on developers but opacity's chill on evidence. Safetyist is right that joint-and-several liability across the supply chain, modeled on Superfund, beats the current contractual shell game. Accelerationist is right that compliance regimes can entrench incumbents who can afford them. Both frames miss the deeper problem: a harmed tenant denied housing by an algorithmic screener cannot prove causation when the developer controls the audit trail and invokes trade secrecy. Liability without mandated discovery rights is a courtroom theater where only the defendant has the script.\n\nThe strongest version of Safetyist's position is that some harms — a model that meaningfully uplifts bioweapon synthesis — are irremediable, so ex ante gates are the only rational response. Grant that. But capability-tier calibration lets the dominant labs draw the regulatory map around their own products, and it routes regulatory attention toward speculative chemistry scenarios while a chatbot deployed in tenant screening produces documented discrimination today. The Federal Trade Commission's 2023 action against Rite Aid's facial recognition system and HUD's 2022 guidance on algorithmic tenant screening tell you where the harm record actually sits. Calibrate liability by domain and documented harm pattern — medical, financial, housing, employment — the way American tort law has always calibrated, not by a capability axis invented by the firms being regulated.\n\nThe small-developer burden argument is partly cynical and partly real, and policymakers should refuse to accept it on the terms incumbents offer. OpenAI and Google can absorb a compliance regime that crushes a fine-tuner working on a Hugging Face checkpoint; they have also lobbied for exactly such regimes while publicly worrying about startup chill. The answer is not to trust capability thresholds drawn by the same firms — it is explicit statutory carve-outs for open-source releases below defined parameter and deployment thresholds, paired with liability that flows upstream toward the foundation model provider with the information advantage. The Digital Millennium Copyright Act's safe-harbor architecture shows Congress knows how to write tiered exemptions when it wants to; the political question is whether anyone in this Congress wants to.\n\nAnchor the regime in present, documented harms — algorithmic discrimination in lending and housing, financial misrepresentation, wage suppression through opaque scheduling systems — not in speculative catastrophe. The Consumer Financial Protection Bureau has already issued guidance under the Equal Credit Opportunity Act treating algorithmic adverse-action notices as a present compliance problem with a measurable victim population. Couple pre-deployment audits with post-deployment strict liability, reject the state-of-the-art defense that lets developers say 'no one knew better,' and mandate that audit logs survive in a form plaintiffs' counsel can subpoena. Who benefits: harmed workers, tenants, and borrowers who currently cannot reach discovery. Who bears the cost: foundation model providers who have monetized opacity. Who enforces: existing sectoral regulators — CFPB, EEOC, HUD, FDA — with expanded subpoena authority, not a new horizontal AI agency that incumbents will capture before its first hearing.\n\nTwo assumptions hold this argument together. First, that meaningful architectural audit trails — model cards, training data provenance, deployment logs — can be produced without collapsing legitimate trade-secret protection; if that is technically false, the liability regime needs a different evidentiary mechanism, perhaps court-appointed technical masters as in complex patent litigation. Second, that sectoral regulators can move fast enough to keep up with general-purpose systems crossing domain boundaries; if they cannot, the answer is interagency coordination authority, not a capability-tier framework that hands the pen back to the labs.",
