olds drawn by the same firms — it is explicit statutory carve-outs for open-source releases below defined parameter and deployment thresholds, paired with liability that flows upstream toward the foundation model provider with the information advantage. The Digital Millennium Copyright Act's safe-harbor architecture shows Congress knows how to write tiered exemptions when it wants to; the political question is whether anyone in this Congress wants to.\n\nAnchor the regime in present, documented harms — algorithmic discrimination in lending and housing, financial misrepresentation, wage suppression through opaque scheduling systems — not in speculative catastrophe. The Consumer Financial Protection Bureau has already issued guidance under the Equal Credit Opportunity Act treating algorithmic adverse-action notices as a present compliance problem with a measurable victim population. Couple pre-deployment audits with post-deployment strict liability, reject the state-of-the-art defense that lets developers say 'no one knew better,' and mandate that audit logs survive in a form plaintiffs' counsel can subpoena. Who benefits: harmed workers, tenants, and borrowers who currently cannot reach discovery. Who bears the cost: foundation model providers who have monetized opacity. Who enforces: existing sectoral regulators — CFPB, EEOC, HUD, FDA — with expanded subpoena authority, not a new horizontal AI agency that incumbents will capture before its first hearing.\n\nTwo assumptions hold this argument together. First, that meaningful architectural audit trails — model cards, training data provenance, deployment logs — can be produced without collapsing legitimate trade-secret protection; if that is technically false, the liability regime needs a different evidentiary mechanism, perhaps court-appointed technical masters as in complex patent litigation. Second, that sectoral regulators can move fast enough to keep up with general-purpose systems crossing domain boundaries; if t