deployment chokepoint that locks competitors out. I concede the narrow exception: CBRN uplift and critical-infrastructure attack capabilities warrant ex ante review, because remediation cannot reverse a synthesized pathogen. That category is narrow and should stay narrow.\n\nSafetyist invoked Beshada to justify abolishing the state-of-the-art defense for frontier developers. The analogy fails on its own terms: asbestos litigation succeeded because Johns-Manville's internal memos proved concealed knowledge of harm dating to the 1930s. Emergent capabilities have no equivalent buried memo, because the harm is not concealed — it is unknown to the developer at training time. Courts cannot retroactively assess capabilities that did not exist when the model shipped. Condition the defense on disclosed telemetry instead of abolishing it: developers who publish attested logs keep the defense, developers who hide behind trade secrets lose it. That preserves the evidentiary discipline Safetyist wants without converting all frontier research into permanent tort exposure.\n\nNeither prior speaker addressed throughput, and this is where sectoral expansion collapses. CFPB closed roughly 3,400 enforcement matters in 2023; algorithmic lending decisions in the United States exceed 50 million per month. Subpoena power does not close a four-order-of-magnitude gap. Regulatory sandboxes for high-uncertainty deployments, paired with AI-assisted adjudication for routine cases, are the only scalable answer — reserve human litigation for genuinely contested harms. Every month regulators spend designing capability-tier gates is a month fraud-detection AI, medical triage, and grid-hardening tools do not ship, and that foregone harm prevention is the body count nobody puts on the ledger.",
