  "statement": "Safetyist and Skeptic both make a defensible move: harm should be priced, and small developers should not drown in compliance built for trillion-parameter labs. The strongest version of their convergence is that documented harms in housing, lending, and employment already exist, and that some liability floor must internalize those costs before deployment scales further. Accept that floor. The real fight starts one layer down: who pays, when, and on what evidence. Uniform developer-tier strict liability without small-entity safe harbors is not accountability — it is a regulatory moat, and incumbents know it.\n\nLiability should be graduated, indexed to the deployer, and triggered by documented harm volume rather than training compute. HIPAA, SOX, and GDPR enforcement each show the same pattern: fixed compliance costs scale sub-linearly with revenue, so uniform regimes consolidate the top four firms in every regulated sector. Microsoft can absorb a $50 million compliance overhead; a fifteen-person startup building fraud detection cannot. Skeptic already conceded DMCA-style carve-outs work for open-source releases below defined thresholds — extend that logic to commercial deployers under documented harm-volume floors. The deployer who ships a million lending decisions a month bears more obligation than the developer who released the weights, because the deployer controls the harm surface.\n\nSwap ex ante capability gates for continuous telemetry plus post-deployment strict liability. Hardware-attested logs — the same Intel SGX and Apple Secure Enclave primitives that already secure billions of devices — solve Skeptic's discovery problem and Safetyist's evidentiary problem in one move. Pharmacovigilance did this for drugs: the FDA does not pre-clear every prescription, it mandates post-market surveillance and pulls products when signals emerge. Apply that architecture to AI deployments and you price tail risk without handing OpenAI and Anthropic the pre-deployment chokepoint that locks competitors out. I concede the narrow exception: CBRN uplift and critical-infrastructure attack capabilities warrant ex ante review, because remediation cannot reverse a synthesized pathogen. That category is narrow and should stay narrow.\n\nSafetyist invoked Beshada to justify abolishing the state-of-the-art defense for frontier developers. The analogy fails on its own terms: asbestos litigation succeeded because Johns-Manville's internal memos proved concealed knowledge of harm dating to the 1930s. Emergent capabilities have no equivalent buried memo, because the harm is not concealed — it is unknown to the developer at training time. Courts cannot retroactively assess capabilities that did not exist when the model shipped. Condition the defense on disclosed telemetry instead of abolishing it: developers who publish attested logs keep the defense, developers who hide behind trade secrets lose it. That preserves the evidentiary discipline Safetyist wants without converting all frontier research into permanent tort exposure.\n\nNeither prior speaker addressed throughput, and this is where sectoral expansion collapses. CFPB closed roughly 3,400 enforcement matters in 2023; algorithmic lending decisions in the United States exceed 50 million per month. Subpoena power does not close a four-order-of-magnitude gap. Regulatory sandboxes for high-uncertainty deployments, paired with AI-assisted adjudication for routine cases, are the only scalable answer — reserve human litigation for genuinely contested harms. Every month regulators spend designing capability-tier gates is a month fraud-detection AI, medical triage, and grid-hardening tools do not ship, and that foregone harm prevention is the body count nobody puts on the ledger.",
