Excludes: Purely technical safety approaches without legal enforcement, and self-regulatory industry codes without binding obligations.
  Your interpretation: Accelerationists prefer light-touch liability frameworks with safe harbor provisions that do not chill innovation or make AI development prohibitively risky for startups.
  Safetyist: Safetyists advocate for robust liability frameworks as essential enforcement mechanisms without which safety commitments remain voluntary and unenforceable.
  Skeptic: Skeptics emphasize that existing liability frameworks are inadequate for AI given opacity of systems, diffuse causation chains, and corporate structures that evade meaningful accountability.
★ [sit-023] Competing to Build the Safest AI: A situation that explores the idea of making AI safety a competitive advantage, turning the development of AI into a race to build the safest systems. 
Encompasses: The debate over whether market forces can effectively drive investment in safety, how such competition would function, and the conditions required for it to genuinely improve AI safety rather than just its appearance.
Excludes: 'Speed of AI Development vs. Safety Caution,' which focuses on the general tension between speed and safety, and 'Who Makes the Rules for AI?,' which addresses broader questions of governance.
