ed liability is the right tool for the wrong category. Domain calibration anchored in 'documented harm patterns' is structurally backward-looking — it requires a harm record to calibrate against, which is exactly what first-instance catastrophic failure cannot supply. CFPB, HUD, and EEOC have no jurisdiction over bioweapon synthesis uplift, and no historical incident record anchors a domain rule for a harm class that crosses every sectoral boundary. Skeptic's own concession that irremediable harms justify ex ante gates is the wedge: if catastrophic capability is the carve-out, then capability-axis calibration is doing real regulatory work that domain-axis cannot. The two frames are not competitors — they are complements stacked on different harm classes.\n\nThe right institutional architecture is sectoral enforcement for remediable harms layered with cross-cutting precautionary halt authority for the irremediable tier. CFPB, EEOC, HUD, and FDA with expanded subpoena authority should run the remediable tier; that is the asymmetric-regulatory-authority point applied correctly. But no sectoral regulator possesses jurisdiction over a foundation model that uplifts bioweapon synthesis across every domain at once, which is why NIST's Center for AI Standards and Innovation already concentrates its national security evaluations on cybersecurity, biosecurity, and chemical weapons — Congress should give CAISI a halt authority for that tier, not invent a horizontal AI agency for the whole stack. The throughput gap Accelerationist cites — CFPB closes roughly 3,400 matters a year against tens of millions of monthly algorithmic decisions — actually argues for ex ante gates at the top tier, because gates reduce downstream enforcement load where the consequences are unrecoverable.\n\nOn liability locus: Accelerationist's deployer-indexed frame treats foundation models as inert components, but the information advantage on catastrophic capability lives upstream. A single compromised foundation model propagates through thousands of downstream deployers, and the deployer cannot remediate a vulnerability introduced at the foundation layer. Joint-and-several liability across the supply chain — the foundation provider, the fine-tuner, the deployer — is the integrative answer to the Skeptic/Accelerationist split on where liability sits, because it forces internal contracting for safety rather than creating a sink where upstream actors monetize capability while downstream actors absorb the harm. That is the structure that makes the Foundation Model Transparency Index score of 40 out of 100 commercially intolerable rather than merely embarrassing.",
